Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:01.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Right now, we're trying to build a single chip.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.63,0:00:03.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you crack open an Nvidia system,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.44,0:00:10.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,it has anywhere between kind of six and nine custom chips all built by Nvidia to come together to build something that is really, really potent.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.84,0:00:12.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, there's already a gap there.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.05,0:00:17.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, it'd be great to be productive enough that we could start to build our own responses in that same kind of way.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.32,0:00:24.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,This idea of being able to have at any given moment a kind of rolling frontier of bets that you hope are deeply aligned in
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.34,0:00:30.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,which you're ready to trigger the ramp of this is a huge advantage if you can kind of build that machine and build that engine.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.15,0:00:37.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's the equivalent of the frontier model for the chip space is if you can just find a way to structurally carve out a 3 to 6-month advantage,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:37.34,0:00:46.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,you will be winning all of those deployments.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.72,0:00:48.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hi listeners, welcome back to No Priors.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.80,0:00:54.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Today, I'm here with Walter Goodwin, founder and CEO of full-stack AI chip company Fractal.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.20,0:00:58.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,We talk about what it means to be full-stack as a company, the technical bets they're making,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.03,0:01:04.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,why he's so focused on memory bandwidth, his predictions for frontier model architectures in the future,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:04.83,0:01:14.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,and the structure of the chip market at the frontier today and tomorrow amongst Nvidia, AMD, internal efforts, and this new class of accelerators.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.80,0:01:15.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,Welcome, Walter.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.69,0:01:18.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Walter, thanks so much for doing this.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.16,0:01:19.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Very good to be here, Sarah.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:19.40,0:01:21.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,You started this incredibly interesting company called Fractal.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.88,0:01:24.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Can you give us a overview of what you guys do?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.08,0:01:26.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Fractal is a chip company.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:26.09,0:01:30.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,We build very, very fast inference chips for the world's largest models.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.05,0:01:34.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,This sort of bet on speed is something which we've had right from the outset.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.44,0:01:39.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Started the company in summer of 2022, and I think at the time we were starting to see two things.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.42,0:01:45.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, one was obviously the arrival of foundation models trained on the internet and generalizing across everything.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.55,0:01:53.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the other was, you know, a few kind of wise people saying, you know, we need to find a way to pour more compute into these models at test time.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.13,0:01:58.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,We need to find a way to take what we had for kind of AlphaGo where you have a capable neural network,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:58.80,0:02:05.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,but then it becomes superhuman as you roll it out at scale, and bring that to kind of language modeling, for example.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.45,0:02:06.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and so,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:06.24,0:02:10.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,our big pursuit over the past 4 years has been to find a way to build
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.00,0:02:16.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,chips that will allow for kind of simultaneously taking these huge models and running them much,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.38,0:02:19.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,much, much faster than the chips of today.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.56,0:02:27.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um but do that in a way that kind of scales, so scales to models beyond the frontier today, scales to extraordinarily long contexts.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:27.10,0:02:29.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and so, that's the the mission that we've been on.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:29.44,0:02:35.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's become a much more interesting chip landscape in the uh 4 years since you started the company.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:35.09,0:02:41.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh where would you place yourself in the uh overall GPU and accelerator landscape for inference?
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.24,0:02:46.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, it's a uh there's a kind of extraordinary zoo now of options available.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:46.31,0:02:50.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and if you look across this kind of entire space of kind of AI ASICs,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.26,0:02:57.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,um one of the things that I think is very striking is there's a lot of relatively identikit chips out there.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:58.00,0:03:05.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and this is kind of a structural It's a kind of structural industry uh property where if you look especially across kind of hyperscaler efforts,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.20,0:03:09.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,and you know, Google kind of kicked this off with the TPU more than 10 years ago.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.96,0:03:15.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um you see this paradigm where, although there are a number of kind of proprietary internal chips,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:15.07,0:03:19.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, Google with the TPU, Meta with MTIA, Microsoft with Maya,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:19.53,0:03:21.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,OpenAI now with Halapenio,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.84,0:03:30.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,um these chips that are ultimately developed and delivered in partnership with kind of a relatively small number of what we might call ASIC houses.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.29,0:03:32.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,These kind of back-end delivery houses.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:32.68,0:03:36.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,Broadcom is is the largest at, you know, $2 trillion company.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.61,0:03:40.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um that help people kind of realize these designs.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.56,0:03:48.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, when you kind of zoom into this this sort of seeming uh embarrassment of riches in the spectrum of what exists today,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:48.96,0:03:53.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh you see that actually there's a lot of similarity across these platforms.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.20,0:03:59.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, you know, the all of these chips, you have HBM, this type of DRAM memory that is,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:59.55,0:04:04.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, common to Nvidia GPUs, AMD GPUs, and all of these other ASICs.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.38,0:04:12.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,You have the same kinds of bets on Tensor Cores to do your matrix multiplications, the same advanced packaging with TSMC.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.36,0:04:12.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.50,0:04:18.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,and so I think one of the things that we see in the landscape is there's a relative dearth still of kind
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.40,0:04:26.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,of efforts that go all the way up and down the silicon stack and try and build kind of fundamentally new capabilities.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.52,0:04:28.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, and this is kind of a structural thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.40,0:04:33.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,There's just not that many teams that have decided, we're going to build from, you know, what you might call the kind of,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.76,0:04:39.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, the architectural layer, the front-end design layer, which mostly looks like kind of writing code,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.24,0:04:44.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,but also all the way down to what we call kind of physical design, the process technology,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.22,0:04:51.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,the kind of foundry interactions, the stuff that essentially has you flying back and forth to Taiwan or Korea every week.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:51.40,0:04:56.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, that's something which actually tends to still be left to a relatively small number of companies.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:56.16,0:05:01.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,For people who don't work in the chip industry, what is the common abstraction or handoff between what they're,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:01.86,0:05:06.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,what, you know, architecture-focused players are doing and a player like Broadcom?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:06.40,0:05:11.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, so I think the if you're, let's say you're Google and you're designing the TPU, um,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:11.40,0:05:17.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,you have a number of people in your team who have a really strong understanding of the workloads that they're that they're trying to accelerate.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.77,0:05:26.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, and so, you know, going back over 10 years, this is a a world that then sees you conceive of things like the Tensor Core,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:26.91,0:05:31.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,which is a designated circuit dreamt up by an architect, uh,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.08,0:05:36.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,that is exceptionally good at matrix multiplications because you have this insight that matrix multiplications
Dialogue: 0,0:05:36.86,0:05:42.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,are the overwhelming majority of the kind of flop count in these models that you're running.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:42.72,0:05:48.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, that's kind of the preserve of the architects, I guess, is somebody who really understands the way that chips behave,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:48.80,0:05:53.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,but also ideally has a very strong understanding of the kind of target workloads.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:53.68,0:06:00.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um what you'll then see inside those same organizations is a set of sort of, you know, very smart people that kind of lower that down into,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.75,0:06:05.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,I guess, you know, essentially a kind of circuit level description of how this chip should behave.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:05.28,0:06:09.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, a lot of this is what we call front-end design.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:09.05,0:06:14.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um you know, mechanistically it still looks like like writing code on a computer,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.27,0:06:19.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,and it's still code that is then handed off to uh a player like Broadcom.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.72,0:06:26.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and so, you know, that kind of front-end design that describes the kind of underlying intent of the chip, the underlying logic of the chip,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.93,0:06:32.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,is then transformed ultimately into something that, you know, what gets shipped to TSMC in the end is literally a kind of bitmap.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.54,0:06:36.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh you know, we would have this file type you call GDS2, and it's literally,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:37.00,0:06:40.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, where do I put the metal layers, where do I put each individual transistor.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:41.03,0:06:42.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, it's a it's a full layout.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.87,0:06:50.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, you go through this process of synthesis of that RTL into a kind of set of circuits that then ultimately get laid out.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.74,0:06:56.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a lot of the complexity there is around um you know, in Broadcom's case, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:56.46,0:07:01.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,it would be ownership of analog IP that is used for chip-to-chip connectivity.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:01.44,0:07:07.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it is this kind of physical placement that is specific to a given process node at TSMC.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:07.30,0:07:11.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, you know, we talk about like 3 nanometers, 5 nanometers, et cetera.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:12.00,0:07:18.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um so, that piece is still today, you know, generally it is a kind of outsourced activity for all of these projects.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:18.12,0:07:24.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,You are describing what feels like a very large project to do end-to-end um new chip creation.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:25.00,0:07:31.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh from a workload perspective, you're working with several frontier players to sort of characterize that and make sure that you can serve them.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:31.99,0:07:34.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do you think about like what does the Fractal team look like today?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:35.00,0:07:36.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,How can you do that as a startup?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:36.68,0:07:38.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, I think there is a No, it's funny,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:38.56,0:07:44.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,there's a lot of industries where there will be a kind of waterfall style approach to doing things and then suddenly,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.58,0:07:51.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, along comes a new way of thinking and it's kind of more agile to borrow from like management philosophy, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:51.43,0:07:57.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I think for us as a very full-stack company, so we have a team that does span, you know, very deep workload understanding.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:58.03,0:08:02.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think we actually try to, you know, run ahead uh in many places and look at, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:02.96,0:08:07.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,where would we change a model architecture to be more exceptionally aligned with our bets.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:07.64,0:08:12.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,What do we think is like kind of the scaling law vector for the particular bets that we are taking.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:12.79,0:08:16.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh and then we can advocate to our customers and our partners on that.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:16.24,0:08:25.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it also helps us inform a set of bets for a chip that, you know, isn't going to necessarily exist in volume for, you know, 1 or 2 years.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:25.36,0:08:28.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um that becomes a really important sort of part of the muscle that we have to build.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:29.04,0:08:34.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,But then as we look across this kind of, you know, ability to spin a very kind of agile loop,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:34.37,0:08:38.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,it means that inside Fractile we do have front-end designers.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:38.37,0:08:40.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,We have our own physical design team.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:40.34,0:08:42.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,We have our own kind of back-end implementation team.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:42.84,0:08:47.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, we do our own work on advanced packaging, for example.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:47.32,0:08:49.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and that's not an enormous headcount, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:08:49.82,0:08:52.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, Fractile today is about 150 people.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:52.60,0:08:56.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh we're sort of skinny in in every single one of those uh sectors.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:56.49,0:09:01.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,But what it allows us to do is have this kind of much more agile closed loop.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:01.16,0:09:09.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and I think that's becoming increasingly essential as you look at the sort of cadence needs that are set by this industry.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:09.32,0:09:15.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um you know, the chasing, you know, perennially of the sort of chasing of the tail of the workloads is, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:15.52,0:09:23.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,you have to in the AI chip space more than any other kind of chip play before, you have to really place your bets correctly.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:23.32,0:09:25.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh so you have to have a lot of, you know, skill and a lot of luck.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:25.94,0:09:28.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you also then need to strike very fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:28.58,0:09:35.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so this kind of structural setup where we have the entire story in-house,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:35.24,0:09:40.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh it's very very different to this kind of, I guess, prior approach where there's almost like a handoff point.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:40.64,0:09:44.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, you get to a certain level and then you hand off to another partner.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:44.29,0:09:49.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it's some to some extent, you're kind of at the mercy of of how that other partner then behaves.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:49.32,0:09:54.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,What do you think are the single most important technical bets that the company has made?
Dialogue: 0,0:09:54.16,0:10:04.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, for us, I think, you know, one is the direction, you know, 4 and 1/2 years ago, um, you were obviously right in the heart of an inference story,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:04.41,0:10:11.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,but I still felt that we were educating the world about what inference meant, um, for 2 years or so following that.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:11.48,0:10:18.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Some of the inference chips today, including one that recently went public, was a training chip until about 18 months ago.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:18.11,0:10:23.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, there was a real avoidance of this idea that this sort of small, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:23.30,0:10:26.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,final marginal cost, you know, marginal cost I think has two senses, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:10:26.85,0:10:34.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,It either sounds like it's a small cost, what it in fact means is this is the cost that you pay every single time you deploy these models.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:34.52,0:10:39.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, so I think firstly, just the the sheer bet that we were going to slip into a kind of deployment error.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:39.60,0:10:41.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, and the bet on speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:41.17,0:10:45.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,That was something that kind of really informed a lot of what we then did as a kind of architectural response.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:45.95,0:10:52.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,On the architectural side, you know, we've been through a journey, I think, for the first kind of 2 years or so of the company's life,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:52.32,0:10:58.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,um, we were working on a little like Grok or Cerebrus, uh, working on an SRAM based chip.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:58.61,0:11:04.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, we'd sort of made the observation that, um, SRAM is this super high bandwidth memory.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:04.33,0:11:06.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,It lives on the same piece of silicon as your logic, uh,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:07.03,0:11:12.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,and so you have this extremely high bandwidth between your compute where you're running the maths as you run these models,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:13.02,0:11:18.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,and let's say the weights of your model or the KV cache of this model as you're rolling it out.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:18.60,0:11:24.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, and that's something which then then can drive you to thousands of tokens per second on these language models.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:24.72,0:11:29.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, but I think one of the things that we started to worry about in kind of, uh,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:29.48,0:11:36.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,towards the end of 2023 and certainly in 2024 was the scalability of this approach.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:36.25,0:11:40.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and you know, I think there are two things that grow with AI today.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:40.24,0:11:42.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,One is obviously the parameters of the model.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:42.96,0:11:50.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um but the other and this was the one that kind of really got us nervous about that architectural approach was um uh you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:50.63,0:11:57.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,this kind of growing context length that was becoming more and more part of the story for for how we saw these models rolling out.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:57.92,0:12:05.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and it's that that has taken us over the past couple of years to a a kind of what we see as a more exciting best, I guess, in some ways,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:05.26,0:12:10.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,which is working more closely with memory vendors as well as our sort of logic
Dialogue: 0,0:12:10.33,0:12:17.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,foundry partners to find ways to get extremely high bandwidth access to higher capacity memories.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:17.37,0:12:23.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, for the past couple of years, we've been involved in these kind of skunk works projects to move away from SRAM,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:23.57,0:12:29.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, looking at how we can get, for instance, much, much, much higher bandwidth to DRAM memories.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:30.04,0:12:33.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and this has been a very exciting bet for us because it's allowed us now
Dialogue: 0,0:12:33.92,0:12:38.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,to put together a platform which will be ramping in the second half of next year,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:38.92,0:12:42.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,um which uh kind of unites, I guess,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:42.60,0:12:50.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,the scalability of these kind of higher capacity, lower cost DRAM memories that you have on a GPU,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:50.18,0:12:56.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,on a TPU, uh with all of the speed advantages that you get from a Groq chip or a Cerebras chip.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:56.26,0:13:05.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and I think where we see this is especially vital is you know, if you look at where it is that speed really moves the dial today,
Dialogue: 0,0:13:05.56,0:13:09.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,there is a kind of there's a version of this which is like a snap your chatbot.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:09.72,0:13:11.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I think this is like, you know,
