Dialogue: 0,0:26:01.24,0:26:03.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,much more important to accelerate those trials as well.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:03.72,0:26:07.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, you know, we are looking internally at, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:07.05,0:26:14.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,what is actually what are in the guts of some of those algorithms and is there a crude approximate way that you could do some of this,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:14.08,0:26:20.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, the I think chip design is not going to change in the final sign-off for quite some time.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:20.63,0:26:29.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's incredibly valuable to have Cadence and Synopsys who've been working with TSMC and the other foundries for decades to build the sort of,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:29.95,0:26:35.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, the final check mark that says this thing is what we call DRC and LVS clean.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:35.57,0:26:39.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,It conforms to the rules that that foundry has set.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:39.40,0:26:43.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,That is actually where, you know, that sort of work is immensely valuable.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:43.39,0:26:49.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,But could you have some kind of fuzzy placement algorithm in the meantime that gets you almost all the way to your final design,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:49.09,0:26:56.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,helps you iterate faster, you know, I think that is exactly where people should be should be doing more work because otherwise that is the bottleneck.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:57.00,0:27:05.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um the other part there and I think this is true of, you know, RSI for AI experiments as well is as we do become, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:05.15,0:27:08.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,as our intelligence levels on the the thinking that then drives that experiment
Dialogue: 0,0:27:09.01,0:27:13.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,gets higher and higher um and as that experiment therefore becomes the bottleneck,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:13.45,0:27:17.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think we do more thinking and this is just generally Fractiles workload bets.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:17.43,0:27:24.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's the reason we're so excited about hard problems and it's kind of the reason that we see Fractile as the chip that
Dialogue: 0,0:27:24.42,0:27:34.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,takes us toward accelerating the solution to hard problems in general is I think it becomes absolutely It's like almost uh you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:34.24,0:27:39.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,a moral compunction to think harder before every single experiment that you fire off.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:40.04,0:27:48.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um I think uh it was maybe Baron Milledge on Dwarkesh recently who said, you know, um that perhaps you could think for a hundred years, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:48.21,0:27:54.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,before you fire off an experiment and then think for another hundred years of human equivalent with these models uh about the results
Dialogue: 0,0:27:54.69,0:27:59.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,of that AI experiment because the experiment in the middle is pretty expensive and takes takes a certain amount of wall clock time.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:00.02,0:28:03.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so I think you get there also with things like chip design where
Dialogue: 0,0:28:03.74,0:28:09.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,there are these kind of fundamental wall clock time bottlenecks for the more conventional algorithms.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:09.14,0:28:16.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're going to be generating a lot of reasoning tokens um before we go and place a given circuit.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:16.28,0:28:25.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,You are making workload bets and then collaborating closely with design partners uh on the model architectural,
Dialogue: 0,0:28:25.82,0:28:29.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, shifts hopefully a little ahead so you can actually do some planning.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:29.22,0:28:30.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,What are you seeing?
Dialogue: 0,0:28:30.20,0:28:34.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,The biggest thing that we're doing, of course, is this kind of speed chasing, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:28:34.35,0:28:38.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Maximizing memory bandwidth uh in order to be able to run these models faster.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:38.88,0:28:46.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and one of the dualities I think you have to span as a chip company is to build something that is both exceptionally good at the kind
Dialogue: 0,0:28:46.36,0:28:49.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,of gamut of workloads that are coming down the pipe under this idea of
Dialogue: 0,0:28:49.77,0:28:56.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,the kind of hardware lottery where these will be trained on HBM-based GPUs or XPUs.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:56.08,0:28:58.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and so it's great that this is then the chip that's exceptional at
Dialogue: 0,0:28:58.78,0:29:03.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,taking kind of today's ultra-aggressive MOE transformers and running them much faster and more efficiently.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:03.92,0:29:10.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um but I think what's exciting about building chips with kind of fundamentally new capabilities is you then also get to explore
Dialogue: 0,0:29:10.21,0:29:18.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,whether there are kind of gravitational pulls that you yourself get to pull the new model landscape in because of those properties.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:18.25,0:29:24.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so for Fractal, you know, this idea of having like 25 times more bandwidth per chip than a than an HBM-based chip,
Dialogue: 0,0:29:24.78,0:29:30.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,we get to explore for instance internally these kind of ideas of like scaling laws for bandwidth basically.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:30.39,0:29:34.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, the scaling laws traditionally we think of them as like flop scaling laws.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:34.37,0:29:40.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, we uh we get better and better performance the more flops we pour into these models at train time and and test time.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:40.76,0:29:47.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and if you look at just like the known landscape of ideas today, we already see some scaling laws for bandwidth.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:47.62,0:29:50.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,So one is uh these mixture of expert models.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:50.54,0:29:56.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's pretty well known that actually ideally we would make them sparser and sparser and sparser.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:56.13,0:30:07.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,So for like iso-intelligence, you will save a ton of flops if you go from being like 1 in 16 sparse on your MOE to 1 in 128, 1 in 256.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:07.92,0:30:09.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um but one of the challenges,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:09.51,0:30:12.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,one of the headwinds to doing that is actually that it becomes
Dialogue: 0,0:30:12.13,0:30:17.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,incredibly prohibitive on today's HBM-based GPUs or XPUs to serve those models efficiently.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:17.88,0:30:20.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,You end up often bandwidth bottlenecks.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:20.66,0:30:26.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,You end up uh serving at really, really low uh MFU, low flops utilization.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:26.28,0:30:34.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and so there are these areas where even as you build for today's world, you get to kind of look at where you can unlock greater capability.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:34.78,0:30:37.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you know, with LLMs today, it's very similar actually with attention.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:37.74,0:30:40.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,There are forms of attention that are less bandwidth hungry.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:40.95,0:30:45.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,They tend to be more flops hungry for a certain level of intelligence.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:45.34,0:30:50.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, one of the things I think we're excited about is as we elevate this other quality,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:50.47,0:30:54.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,this property of memory bandwidth, which hasn't really been scaled so much on chips recently.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:54.50,0:30:58.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,We've scaled flops like a millionfold in the last 20 years.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:58.48,0:31:02.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Memory bandwidth has gone up about 40x in the same time frame.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:02.20,0:31:05.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, as you scale that frontier, you get to conserve more of the other thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:05.87,0:31:11.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,We get to reduce the number of flops that we're using on these models to get to a certain level of intelligence.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:11.44,0:31:16.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, that's a kind of really a multiplying factor on global throughput for these models.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:16.72,0:31:21.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'll resist asking you if that changes people's ideas about how they should train for now.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:21.36,0:31:24.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,One last question for you just on on market structure here.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:24.55,0:31:27.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's very unclear to me, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:27.72,0:31:35.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,5 years from now what a large AI player or a hyperscaler buys in terms
Dialogue: 0,0:31:35.39,0:31:40.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,of or what what chips they consume between the Nvidia and AMD's of the world,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:40.60,0:31:46.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,the new accelerator class, and you know, four at least four of these players have their own internal efforts.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:46.81,0:31:48.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, how do you make sense of this?
Dialogue: 0,0:31:48.52,0:31:52.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, I think that today we see uh you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:53.03,0:32:01.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,behavior where really everybody who is deploying at scale is kind of trying to deploy as many separate platforms as they can.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:01.20,0:32:05.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think one thing that will remain the case is that there will be a real need
Dialogue: 0,0:32:05.52,0:32:12.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,for kind of a diversity of supply as something like compute becomes so existential to these players.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:12.30,0:32:18.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,There's a bit of a joke today that the sort of first party efforts, their primary purpose is to reduce the price that people pay Nvidia.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:18.95,0:32:24.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,And there may be some truth to that as well because those efforts are kind of architecturally quite similar, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:32:24.14,0:32:29.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, I'd say it's a bet that is not on enabling a fundamental capability that those other chips cannot enable.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:29.64,0:32:32.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, there I think it is this kind of gamesmanship of, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:32.79,0:32:39.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,can we build some of this over here or can I buy something from AMD so that maybe I then get a better price from Nvidia or and so on.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:39.50,0:32:43.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and it and it play also for kind of aggregate capacity and a bit of control.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:43.80,0:32:49.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think as you then see this move into chips that actually give new capabilities, you know, that's the place where, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:49.79,0:32:55.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,I suddenly see for what we're doing a real need for everybody that wants to deploy AI at the frontier,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:55.64,0:33:00.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,they have to have some solution to running these models orders of magnitude faster.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:00.25,0:33:08.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um you know, for the frontier labs, there's this window of kind of premium capability, premium intelligence that is their entire raison d'être.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:08.48,0:33:11.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, otherwise we'd all be using Kimmy models all the time.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:11.84,0:33:15.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and so as you do have this pressure from behind from open source, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:33:15.72,0:33:22.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,it does become clearly even more important for folks who want to deploy at the frontier to have every aspect of what that means.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:22.71,0:33:29.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, the best model weights, but also then the fastest deployment, so you can do the most reasoning in the in the shortest amount of time.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:29.11,0:33:29.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so,
Dialogue: 0,0:33:29.57,0:33:35.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think this kind of premium end of of of the chip space where you're optimizing for
Dialogue: 0,0:33:35.21,0:33:39.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,speed over everything else becomes a kind of very important part of this uh part of this world.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:39.91,0:33:46.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think one thing that is notable about, you know, the dynamic between a chip vendor and a frontier lab,
Dialogue: 0,0:33:46.85,0:33:51.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, if you look at Fractal, we try to be as aligned as possible with the needs of the frontier.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:51.91,0:33:54.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,We try to look a bit like some of those teams as well.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:54.44,0:33:57.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,We have people that really think deeply about these workloads and so on.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:57.88,0:34:02.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, one of the cases that I think we sometimes need to make is explaining why we believe that over,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:02.76,0:34:09.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, multiple decades the world sustains some kind of frontier third-party chip players.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:09.05,0:34:12.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, why does this not just roll up into these into these labs?
Dialogue: 0,0:34:12.76,0:34:20.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and I think that comes down to the kind of asymmetric game that all of these labs and frontier players have to play,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:20.12,0:34:26.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh where they're exposed to an enormous risk if they go all in on a hardware bet.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:26.65,0:34:29.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um Suppose I'm, you know, I'm lab one,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:29.87,0:34:36.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh and I've gone all in on some proprietary silicon and then lab two discovers some new computational breakthrough that delivers
Dialogue: 0,0:34:36.93,0:34:43.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,far better computational efficiencies for the same level of intelligence but it only works on the chip that they've decided to deploy.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:43.69,0:34:51.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,I could die in the nine months before I get to deploy enough of that chip that I've also now get into that kind of 5x in computational efficiency.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:51.16,0:34:58.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so there is a really urgent need for these chips to actually deploy the same these companies to deploy the same platforms as one another.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:58.82,0:35:03.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,They're playing different games so right they're trying to now compete you know in the model layer.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:03.60,0:35:10.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think having deeply differentiated bets and going all in on those differentiated bets at the chip layer
Dialogue: 0,0:35:10.49,0:35:14.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,is sort of an irrational and a very dangerous move for anybody that is playing at the frontier.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:14.76,0:35:16.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, I think that's a great place to end.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:16.15,0:35:17.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thanks so much Walter.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:17.20,0:35:20.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thanks very much Sarah.
